   Example:

   input: [{x=1.5, y=0.1}, {x=-3, y=1.1}, {x=5, y=9}, {x=1.5, y=-1}, {x=3, y=-7}, {x=0.1, y=2}]
   output: [3, 1, 0, 2]
*/
void countQuadrants(std::vector<Point> const& points, std::array<size_t, 4> &bins) {
   for (auto const& point : points) {
      if (point.x >= 0) {
            if (point.y >= 0) {
                bins[0] += 1;
            } else {
                bins[3] += 1;
            }
      } else {
            if (point.y >= 0) {
                bins[1] += 1;
            } else {
                bins[2] += 1;
            }
      }
   }
}
```

The original code checks the same conditions multiple times, which is unnecessary. By checking the sign of `point.x` once and then using it to decide which condition to check for `point.y`, we can eliminate some redundant checks. This reduces the number of conditional branches and improves the performance of the code.

